WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:04.350
- [Interviewer] So, Clarence.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:05.183
- [Clarence] Yeah?

00:00:05.183 --> 00:00:06.016
- [Interviewer] Are you a hillbilly?

00:00:06.016 --> 00:00:06.849
- Oh yeah, I'm a hillbilly.

00:00:06.849 --> 00:00:07.810
I'm from the hills, man.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:09.767
From the hill over there
I go up on the hills

00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:11.555
get a beer and go and have in here

00:00:11.555 --> 00:00:14.700
like an old gray head
livin' in the middle, man.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:15.933
He ain't really gonna bring that.

00:00:16.926 --> 00:00:18.240
Hiding in the mountains,
and hiding in the dark, man.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.279
Keep out of my kin, man.

00:00:21.279 --> 00:00:22.901
High up here, that's right.

00:00:22.901 --> 00:00:24.976
It's like, I made him proud about that.

00:00:24.976 --> 00:00:26.422
I'm a hillbilly, man.

00:00:26.422 --> 00:00:27.860
I'm from the Appalachian mountains

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:29.072
Hiding in the dirt here.

00:00:29.072 --> 00:00:30.930
Hillbillies up on a
hill, up on Jesus' hill.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.403
Gets on goin' south.

00:00:33.583 --> 00:00:34.540
Bringing it right here.

00:00:35.581 --> 00:00:37.562
He was talking about the Lord.

00:00:37.562 --> 00:00:38.843
He had my hopes up.

00:00:38.843 --> 00:00:40.442
One time I took a drink
I was hallucinating.

00:00:40.442 --> 00:00:42.192
I'm a hillbilly, man.

00:00:44.549 --> 00:00:47.382
I got my same daddy's
song I got used to them.

00:00:47.382 --> 00:00:48.493
He freezed them up.

00:00:49.876 --> 00:00:51.430
- [Interviewer] So, Clarence.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:52.263
- Okay?

00:00:52.263 --> 00:00:53.096
- [Interviewer] Clarence,
where'd you grow up?

00:00:53.096 --> 00:00:53.929
Where are you from originally?

00:00:53.929 --> 00:00:55.950
- Well, I'm from Leslie
County, Kentucky, right there.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:59.590
Hyden, Kentucky, right there, and...

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:01.050
- [Interviewer] You grew
up with both your parents?

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:02.740
- I live with, my grand dad raised me

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.245
from the time I came from the hospital

00:01:04.245 --> 00:01:05.078
until I was six,

00:01:05.078 --> 00:01:06.078
and then my grandma passed on.

00:01:06.078 --> 00:01:08.110
And went and stayed at
my dad and mom's house

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:09.026
until I was about eight,

00:01:09.026 --> 00:01:09.859
and my mom and dad got divorced,

00:01:09.859 --> 00:01:11.120
and I went to,

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.700
I come up here to Boylston,
1418 Boylston Road,

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.250
Leslie Country, Kentucky right there.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:16.790
And I stayed here until I was thirteen

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:18.141
and my grandpa took me
back home right there.

00:01:18.141 --> 00:01:19.330
And I stayed with him until

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.180
I got a job when I was about
nineteen at the Sav-a-Lot

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:23.230
working as a butcher.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.233
- [Interviewer] What kind of jobs did you

00:01:25.233 --> 00:01:26.066
have throughout your life?

00:01:26.066 --> 00:01:29.534
- Well, I only, I worked at
the Sav-a-Lot for a few years

00:01:29.534 --> 00:01:32.640
as a butcher, as the head butcher,

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:33.473
the boss of the meat department,

00:01:33.473 --> 00:01:36.590
and then I worked a little factory job

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:40.633
down in Jackson as a line worker there on.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:43.705
I got disabled there,

00:01:43.705 --> 00:01:47.393
and I started drawing my social security.

00:01:47.393 --> 00:01:49.487
I just got about seven or eight years in,

00:01:49.487 --> 00:01:50.654
and I started drawing social security.

00:01:50.654 --> 00:01:52.450
- [Interviewer] Did you
ever work in a coal mine

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:53.283
like a lot of guys do?

00:01:53.283 --> 00:01:54.666
- No, I never worked in a coal mine, no.

00:01:54.666 --> 00:01:56.326
They're about gone right here.

00:01:56.326 --> 00:01:57.159
Yep, yep.

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:00.727
When I was a little boy there

00:02:00.727 --> 00:02:04.120
I wanted me a guitar, and Dad
got me about seven or eight,

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:04.953
seven I believe it was.

00:02:04.953 --> 00:02:07.560
And I been playin' music and writing songs

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.203
ever since I was about seven or eight.

00:02:09.203 --> 00:02:10.724
- [Interviewer] Is
there a lot of musicians

00:02:10.724 --> 00:02:11.637
in your family?

00:02:11.637 --> 00:02:15.040
- Yeah, yeah, I got a cousin
in Indiana right there.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.411
He's got a southern rock band.

00:02:17.411 --> 00:02:20.473
My grand dad used to be a banjo picker.

00:02:21.821 --> 00:02:23.553
And he played a banjo,

00:02:24.436 --> 00:02:26.980
and I got a cousin, John.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:27.813
He's like me.

00:02:27.813 --> 00:02:29.850
He can hear a song and just pick it up

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:30.833
and play it by ear.

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:33.700
There's a bunch of musicians in my family.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.860
My first thing is
fighting chickens, though.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:37.810
I been fighting chickens since
I was about ten right there.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:38.770
- [Interviewer] So like cock fighting?

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.127
- Yeah, cock fighting, yeah, and-

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:41.960
(clears throat)

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:42.793
- [Interviewer] Is that what
you do for a living now?

00:02:42.793 --> 00:02:43.660
- No, I don't do it for a living.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:44.962
I do it for fun.

00:02:44.962 --> 00:02:46.416
I raise and sell 'em right there.

00:02:46.416 --> 00:02:47.670
'Cause you take 'em by,

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:50.571
and Peter, right there.

00:02:50.571 --> 00:02:51.710
- [Interviewer] (interrupting)
What does a cock fighting

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:52.780
rooster get ya?

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.276
- Well I've sold some for
four to seven hundred dollars

00:02:55.276 --> 00:02:56.347
a piece right there.

00:02:56.347 --> 00:02:57.180
- [Interviewer] How much?

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:58.100
- For hundred to a thousand,

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.054
you take a thousand dollars in Louisiana,

00:03:00.054 --> 00:03:00.928
where it's legal right there.

00:03:00.928 --> 00:03:01.967
At a thousand dollars each,

00:03:01.967 --> 00:03:03.725
you sell ten or fifteen,

00:03:03.725 --> 00:03:05.400
that's ten or fifteen thousand
dollars every weekend.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.840
- [Interviewer] So cock
fighting is legal in Louisiana?

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:08.673
- [Clarence] Louisiana and Oklahoma.

00:03:08.673 --> 00:03:09.980
- [Interviewer] But here
in Kentucky it's not?

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:11.037
- [Clarence] Yeah, about two months ago

00:03:11.037 --> 00:03:15.936
they fly drones and over together
and film 'em right there,

00:03:15.936 --> 00:03:17.052
(chickens clucking)

00:03:17.052 --> 00:03:19.950
and take 'em to court and
everything for fighting chickens,

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:20.783
and they shut 'em down.

00:03:20.783 --> 00:03:21.770
I used to get a gamecock management,

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:23.137
about two months ago

00:03:23.137 --> 00:03:26.010
they put some in a a magazine
and everything right there.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:26.843
Yeah, they shut it down.

00:03:26.843 --> 00:03:28.065
It's illegal since 1976

00:03:28.065 --> 00:03:29.504
it's against the law to fight chickens.

00:03:29.504 --> 00:03:31.964
In 1834 right there in England right there

00:03:31.964 --> 00:03:33.710
they shut it down right there.

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:34.801
They outlawed it in England, right there.

00:03:34.801 --> 00:03:35.974
In 1976 they shut it down over here.

00:03:35.974 --> 00:03:38.070
- [Interviewer] So you raise and sell?

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:39.492
- Yeah, raise and sell 'em.

00:03:39.492 --> 00:03:40.325
Sometimes if you raise 'em fine,

00:03:40.325 --> 00:03:41.650
you can send them to the Philippines

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:45.362
and Sunset, Louisiana and
Mexico where it's legal at.

00:03:45.362 --> 00:03:47.507
If they catch you here
you get about 14 months,

00:03:47.507 --> 00:03:49.630
14 months for fighting
chickens as a first offense.

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:50.683
- [Interviewer] So tell me about

00:03:50.683 --> 00:03:54.130
what is it about roosters that
they like to kill each other?

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:56.281
- That's just like, you know boxers?

00:03:56.281 --> 00:03:59.073
It's like a boxer or, actually,

00:03:59.073 --> 00:04:01.236
they have to be like a hound.

00:04:01.236 --> 00:04:02.069
They can be on the run,

00:04:02.069 --> 00:04:03.735
and when the start showing,

00:04:03.735 --> 00:04:05.101
well, that's what they do.

00:04:05.101 --> 00:04:06.487
They go out.

00:04:06.487 --> 00:04:07.747
- [Interviewer] Yeah, I've seen it before

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:09.185
where if they're just
exposed to each other,

00:04:09.185 --> 00:04:10.361
they're gonna try to kill each other.

00:04:10.361 --> 00:04:13.091
- Yeah, that's basically it.

00:04:13.091 --> 00:04:14.309
They're the dominant male.

00:04:14.309 --> 00:04:15.142
They dominate.

00:04:15.142 --> 00:04:16.410
They have to be the boss or die.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.493
That's their part, and...

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:24.580
- [Interviewer] So how
much schooling did you get?

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:26.450
- I went about, I was
on the five year plan,

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:27.960
so I was like three
credits from graduating,

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:31.677
so I went to the adult
learning center right there.

00:04:31.677 --> 00:04:33.710
It was an adult learning
center right there.

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:34.543
- [Interviewer] So you eventually

00:04:34.543 --> 00:04:35.376
got a high school education.

00:04:35.376 --> 00:04:36.587
- G.E.D. General education, yeah.

00:04:36.587 --> 00:04:38.881
Yeah, and I mostly studied,
I didn't go to college,

00:04:38.881 --> 00:04:40.896
but I mostly studied anatomy and breeding

00:04:40.896 --> 00:04:43.807
of chickens and music, guitars and singin'

00:04:43.807 --> 00:04:44.907
and things like that.

00:04:44.907 --> 00:04:46.188
And I like martial arts.

00:04:46.188 --> 00:04:48.290
Karate and stuff, yeah,
that's what I like.

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:49.820
- [Interviewer] What do
you do for money now?

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:51.360
- I got social security right there

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:52.230
and sellin' chickens.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:53.063
That's what I'm doin'.

00:04:53.063 --> 00:04:53.896
I'm retired.

00:04:53.896 --> 00:04:54.920
- [Interviewer] How much
money do you get a month?

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:56.440
- Oh.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:57.532
About 15,000 a year.

00:04:57.532 --> 00:04:58.851
About 1200 dollars a month.

00:04:58.851 --> 00:05:01.174
That's all they give you.

00:05:01.174 --> 00:05:02.007
- So 15,000 a year?

00:05:02.007 --> 00:05:03.333
- About 14,000 dollars a year.

00:05:03.333 --> 00:05:06.333
About 12, 14,000 dollars about, yep.

00:05:07.339 --> 00:05:08.172
Yeah, social security is down.

00:05:08.172 --> 00:05:09.478
- So, this part of the Appalachia

00:05:09.478 --> 00:05:10.881
is one of the poorest
parts of the country?

00:05:10.881 --> 00:05:12.210
- Yeah, there ain't no jobs out here.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:14.170
If you want to get a job
you got to go like two hours

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:16.030
about an hour and a half drive to London

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:17.145
or way down to Jackson

00:05:17.145 --> 00:05:18.993
where all there is is
coal mining or logging,

00:05:18.993 --> 00:05:20.317
and coal mining is about gone.

00:05:20.317 --> 00:05:23.385
When Obama started the war coal,

00:05:23.385 --> 00:05:25.377
a lot of coal companies that were busy,

00:05:25.377 --> 00:05:26.940
they shut down.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:28.558
They make you put a catalytic
converter on your heat stove

00:05:28.558 --> 00:05:31.900
if you got a heat stove.

00:05:32.766 --> 00:05:33.599
Pollution.

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:34.920
They pollute the air right there.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:36.410
- [Interviewer] Yeah, so
coal mining is fading away,

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:37.880
and there's really no other options.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:40.270
No company wants to open up a factory here

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:41.140
'cause it's so remote.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:42.913
- Yeah, it's so remote here.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:45.795
If we get a factory, people have jobs.

00:05:45.795 --> 00:05:49.310
Most people, they drive
and hour and a half

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:51.000
and there ain't nobody around.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.301
'Cause like right here,

00:05:53.301 --> 00:05:54.338
it's almost thirty and a half miles

00:05:54.338 --> 00:05:55.840
down Boylston Road right there,

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:57.761
and there's one or two houses next door

00:05:57.761 --> 00:05:58.971
and there's nothin' out there.

00:05:58.971 --> 00:06:00.763
I went to the store right there.

00:06:01.801 --> 00:06:02.634
They made it a game reserve around here,

00:06:02.634 --> 00:06:03.521
once they get done over there.

00:06:03.521 --> 00:06:05.675
There ain't nothin' but game and animals.

00:06:05.675 --> 00:06:08.925
Nobody live round here or nothin', yup.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:12.020
- [Interviewer] So the median income

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:13.540
of some of the counties here,

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:15.899
it was higher in 1979 then it is today.

00:06:15.899 --> 00:06:17.232
- [Clarence] Yeah, yeah.

00:06:17.232 --> 00:06:19.140
- [Interviewer] It seems
like this way of life

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:20.143
is just fading away.

00:06:20.143 --> 00:06:21.728
- [Clarence] Yeah, it used to be that

00:06:21.728 --> 00:06:23.297
you start workin' at one coal company

00:06:23.297 --> 00:06:26.330
and the average pay last
time, my dad worked,

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:27.163
I was like nine,

00:06:27.163 --> 00:06:29.120
he made about 11, 11
somethin' an hour right there,

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.421
and now nobody pays.

00:06:30.421 --> 00:06:33.700
They start you at like 20, 21
dollars an hour right there,

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.419
and daddy said it used to
be you could be workin'

00:06:35.419 --> 00:06:36.523
at one coal company right there,

00:06:36.523 --> 00:06:39.390
and quite and go down
to the next coal company

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:42.083
would hire you and give you a job,

00:06:42.083 --> 00:06:45.717
but now if you take a job at a
restaurant or a grocery store

00:06:45.717 --> 00:06:49.583
or somethin' like that,
loggers or a coal company,

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:51.846
the opportunities are gone.

00:06:51.846 --> 00:06:52.679
It takes like 20 years.

00:06:52.679 --> 00:06:54.790
It takes about 20-25 years

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:56.667
before you been impoverished right there.

00:06:56.667 --> 00:06:57.500
- [Interviewer] What
are your favorite things

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:58.817
about Appalachia?

00:06:58.817 --> 00:07:01.410
- [Clarence] Well, the
mountains, the game,

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:03.286
and just the climate here.

00:07:03.286 --> 00:07:04.791
It's a good place to live there, you know,

00:07:04.791 --> 00:07:05.810
if you can find a way
make a living right there.

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:08.103
- [Interviewer] What are the
worst things about living here?

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:11.183
- The snow in winter.

00:07:11.183 --> 00:07:12.016
It's cold,

00:07:12.016 --> 00:07:13.517
but you get yourself some wheel chains

00:07:13.517 --> 00:07:17.840
and put them on, I mean,
you can go anywhere.

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:18.673
You can go dig yourself out of here.

00:07:18.673 --> 00:07:20.743
Just diggin' in ice like snow places.

00:07:22.296 --> 00:07:24.448
When I started settlin',
they said the first thing

00:07:24.448 --> 00:07:27.051
I want you to do is go
to get a wheel chain,

00:07:27.051 --> 00:07:29.981
when it snows, whatever, it don't matter.

00:07:29.981 --> 00:07:31.409
I got myself a wheel chain,

00:07:31.409 --> 00:07:34.659
I can pass forward and drive and leave.

00:07:36.253 --> 00:07:40.340
Now, I worked, someone said, someone said

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:43.060
the turbo cans over there, they come by

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:45.840
and show me how to place the
piece and everything to do.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:46.830
He checked me out.

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:47.663
He got to the store and go up front,

00:07:47.663 --> 00:07:51.530
he said if you think its on over there.

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:52.363
I'll run it.

00:07:54.753 --> 00:07:56.166
- [Interviewer] Have you traveled before?

00:07:56.166 --> 00:07:57.140
- I've traveled.

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:57.973
I've been up north.

00:07:57.973 --> 00:08:02.230
Indiana, Ohio, the great
Smoky Mountains of Tennessee,

00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:03.800
Dollywood down there, you know.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:05.250
I've been over to Virginia.

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:08.070
The furthest west I been is Somerset

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:09.214
about three counties over.

00:08:09.214 --> 00:08:11.693
Yup, I ain't been very far.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:15.368
- [Interviewer] Do you see

00:08:15.368 --> 00:08:17.640
the technology that's
taking over the world

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:20.060
is kind of leaving Appalachia behind?

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:21.623
- Yeah, well, yeah.

00:08:25.323 --> 00:08:27.561
It's about an hour and a half drive

00:08:27.561 --> 00:08:31.478
down I-75 in the hub of
eastern Kentucky, I-75.

00:08:33.746 --> 00:08:34.579
You go there,

00:08:34.579 --> 00:08:35.412
and they'll start you off at a factory

00:08:35.412 --> 00:08:38.580
at 14 dollars an hour
Dow Chemical factory.

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:41.563
That's where my cousin, all my cousins,

00:08:41.563 --> 00:08:42.396
that's what they did.

00:08:42.396 --> 00:08:44.765
They got a place down
there to be close to work.

00:08:44.765 --> 00:08:46.340
'Cause if you drive an hour and a half

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:49.154
and an hour and a half back,
right there, you get tired.

00:08:49.154 --> 00:08:50.330
If you don't get plenty of rest,

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:52.178
you feel like hell forever
right there I mean.

00:08:52.178 --> 00:08:54.573
So, they just moved right there.

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:57.210
- [Interviewer] Did you find having,

00:08:57.210 --> 00:08:58.540
you know, your accent is very thick.

00:08:58.540 --> 00:09:00.150
The Appalachian accent is so thick

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:01.450
that when you go to places

